<article class="env-lemma" id="0GIQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GIQ">Lemma <span data-tag="0GIQ">29.55.12</span>.</a> Let $X$ be an irreducible, geometrically unibranch scheme. The normalization morphism $\nu : X^\nu \to X$ is a universal homeomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have to show that $\nu $ is integral, universally injective, and surjective, see Lemma <a href="/tag/04DF" data-tag="04DF">29.46.5</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/035Q" data-tag="035Q">29.55.5</a> the morphism $\nu $ is integral. Let $x \in X$ and set $A = \mathcal{O}_{X, x}$. Since $X$ is irreducible we see that $A$ has a single minimal prime $\mathfrak p$ and $A_{red} = A/\mathfrak p$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0C3B" data-tag="0C3B">29.55.4</a> the stalk $A' = (\nu _*\mathcal{O}_{X^\nu })_ x$ is the integral closure of $A$ in the fraction field of $A_{red}$. By More on Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/0BPZ" data-tag="0BPZ">15.108.1</a> we see that $A'$ has a single prime $\mathfrak m'$ lying over $\mathfrak m_ x \subset A$ and $\kappa (\mathfrak m')/\kappa (x)$ is purely inseparable. Hence $\nu $ is bijective (hence surjective) and universally injective by Lemma <a href="/tag/01S4" data-tag="01S4">29.10.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>